<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Frankish Kingdom of Soissons</h1><p class="st-meta">511 CE – 561 CE · capital Soissons</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Merovingian kingdom of Chlothar I, who from his base at Soissons gradually reunited all the Frankish lands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> died in 511, his youngest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> received the old Frankish heartland in the north, between the Somme and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, the former seat of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/domain-of-soissons">Domain of Soissons</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar</a> was the most ruthless of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>' sons. In 524 he personally killed two of his young nephews to seize part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-orleans">Kingdom of Orléans</a>. He shared in the conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-burgundians">Kingdom of the Burgundians</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, where he took the princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a> as his wife.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Through the deaths of his brothers and nephew, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar</a> inherited <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/austrasia">Austrasia</a> in 555 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 558, reuniting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks</a>. He died in 561 and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-medard">Saint-Médard</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilperic-i">Chilperic I</a> received Soissons, the core of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/neustria">Neustria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/frankish-kingdom-of-soissons.jpg" alt="Frankish Kingdom of Soissons" width="320" height="333"><figcaption>Image: Publius-Vergilius-Maro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Gaul,_476_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>511 CE – 561 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Soissons</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chlothar I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Frankish, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Soissons</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=536&amp;focus=frankish-kingdom-of-soissons" aria-label="Frankish Kingdom of Soissons on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4757 456 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Frankish Kingdom of Soissons lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5077 712L5054 728M5077 712L5077 712"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5077 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5077 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5077 712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Soissons. <a href="/atlas?year=536&amp;focus=frankish-kingdom-of-soissons">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
